<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōjō Ujiyasu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1515">1515</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hojo Ujiyasu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hōjō Ujiyasu was the third head of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a> and one of the leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> warlords. His night attack at Kawagoe in 1546 broke a coalition of the Uesugi and made the Hōjō masters of the Kantō. He carried out land surveys and tax reforms and defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu.jpg" alt="Hōjō Ujiyasu" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ujiyasu_Hojo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ujiyasu, whose childhood name was Chiyomaru, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujitsuna">Hōjō Ujitsuna</a>. He fought his first battle at fifteen, against Uesugi Tomooki of the Ōgigayatsu Uesugi clan at the Battle of Ozawahara in 1530. His only known wife, Zuikei-in, was a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a>. When his father died in 1541, enemies of the Hōjō tried to seize their strongholds, and the almost constant warfare pushed Ujiyasu to reorganise the administration of his lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He ordered cadastral surveys in 1542 and 1543 and in 1550 overhauled the Kandaka taxation system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>, the town of his home castle, grew into a trading centre as its roads were redirected and an artisans' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> was set up in the castle town, while post stations and markets spread through Hōjō lands.</p><p>At Kawagoe the garrison of Hōjō Tsunashige, about 3,000 men, faced a besieging army of Uesugi Tomosada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa">Uesugi Norimasa</a> and the Koga Kubo Ashikaga Haruuji said to number 80,000. Ujiyasu came with 8,000, used ninja to learn the enemy's plans, and attacked by night with men who wore light armour and were ordered not to stop to take heads. The victory ended the Ōgigayatsu Uesugi line, and many Kantō warlords became Hōjō vassals. In 1551 he drove Norimasa from Hirai Castle to Echigo, into the care of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Nagao Kagetora</a>, the later Uesugi Kenshin.</p><p>Ujiyasu retired in 1559, at 45, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujimasa">Hōjō Ujimasa</a> became formal head of the family, but he remained active. Kenshin besieged Odawara Castle in 1561 and burned the town before withdrawing after two months. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a>, Ujiyasu retook Matsuyama Castle in 1563 and won the Battle of Kōnodai against Satomi Yoshihiro in 1564, extending Hōjō rule over Sagami, Izu, Musashi, Shimōsa and Kazusa. Satake Yoshishige checked him at the Battle of Numajiri in 1567.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shingen turned on the Hōjō in 1568 and in 1569 burned the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a>, before Ujiyasu's sons Ujiteru and Ujikuni attacked his withdrawing army at Mimasetoge. Ujiyasu then made peace with both rivals, letting his son Hōjō Saburō be adopted by the childless Kenshin and accepting Shingen's hold on Suruga. He died on 21 October 1571, leaving his domains to Ujimasa in a relatively favourable position. He was known as the Lion of Sagami.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hōjō Ujiyasu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hojo Ujiyasu</b> won a celebrated victory at Kawagoe in 1546 and expanded across the Kanto, resisting attacks by Uesugi Kenshin and Takeda Shingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Odawara Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Odawara Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.25,139.15&amp;year=1551" aria-label="Odawara Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8072 889 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hōjō Ujiyasu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8522 1159h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8522 1159h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.25,139.15&amp;year=1551">What was here in 1551? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
